\title{Coarse-Response Ceilings for Rank Correlation\\ Against a Geometric Tie Spectrum}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{24 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $T$ be a statistic whose tie profile on a sample of size $n$ is geometric with ratio $1/q$ --- the canonical example being the trailing-zero count (the $q$-adic valuation) of a uniformly drawn integer. We develop a complete theory of the attainable Spearman rank correlation between such a $T$ and an arbitrary downstream response, resolved by the response's own granularity.

Four results organise the theory. First, a \emph{coarse-response ceiling}: a two-valued response marking $K$ of $n$ items satisfies $\rho^2 \le nK(n-K)/(4\,\ssb)$, where $\ssb$ is the between-block sum of squares of the midranks of $T$, with equality exactly when the marked set is a top segment of the $T$-order. On the dyadic profile this specialises to the \emph{rate parabola} $\rho^2_{\max}(p) = \tfrac72 p(1-p)\,n^3/(n^3-1)$. Second, a \emph{nested-ties identity}: for any response constant on groups of consecutive tie blocks, $\rho^2 = \ssb(\text{coarse})/\ssb(\text{fine})$ exactly --- the squared correlation is the surviving fraction of between-block variance. This identity subsumes both the classical $6/7$ tie-attenuation ceiling and the rate parabola. Third, a \emph{bulk/tip asymmetry}: merging the bottom $1-2^{-t}$ of the $T$-scale caps $\rho^2$ at $\bigl(\tfrac72(2^t-1)2^t 8^{b-t} + 8^{b-t}-1\bigr)/(8^b-1)$, which tends to $197/512 \approx 0.3848$ at $t=3$, whereas merging the entire top $2^{-t}$ leaves $\rho^2 = (8^b - 8^{b-t})/(8^b-1) > 7/8$ for every $t$. Fourth, a \emph{shape-constant theorem}: for the ratio-$1/q$ spectrum, $\ssb = q(n^3-1)/(4(q^2+q+1))$ and the ceiling constant is $C(q) = q+1+1/q$, strictly increasing; the dyadic case $C(2) = 7/2$ is the smallest, making the binary regime the most restrictive member of the family.

The theory is calibrated against a concrete measurement: rank correlations of $0.7192$, $0.7202$, $0.7198$ between the trailing-zero count and a downstream relation rate on uniform draws of exact bit-length $48$, at a mean relation rate of $12.5\%$. The ceilings exclude a two-valued response at that rate (cap $0.6187$), exclude every response blind inside the $87.5\%$ no-relation bulk (cap $\rho^2 = 0.3848$ against a measured $\rho^2 \approx 0.5172$), locate the blindness threshold sharply between depths $t=2$ and $t=3$, and permit total blindness on the top half of the scale. A uniform bound $\lvert\rho^2_{\max}(p,b) - \tfrac72 p(1-p)\rvert \le 2\cdot 8^{-b}$ explains, algebraically, the observed flatness of the measurement across bit-length scans.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Spearman rank correlation; tie profile; $2$-adic valuation; midranks; between-group sum of squares; geometric tie spectrum; resolution ladder; coarse response.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The measurement}

Draw integers uniformly at random with exact bit-length $48$. For each draw record $T$, the number of trailing zero bits --- equivalently, the $2$-adic valuation. Feed the draw into a downstream process which either produces a certain structural relation or does not; over the ensemble it produces one with mean rate $12.5\%$.

Across three independent seeds the Spearman rank correlation between $T$ and the relation rate is
\[
  \rho = 0.7192, \qquad 0.7202, \qquad 0.7198,
\]
a spread of $0.001$, and $T$ outperforms a popcount (``number of $1$ bits'') baseline by $+0.098$ to $+0.145$ throughout. The correlation is stable, strong, and unexplained.

\subsection{The question}

The correlation is a single scalar; the mechanism producing the relation rate is unknown. What can one scalar establish about an unknown mechanism? A great deal, provided one exploits the \emph{tie structure} of $T$. Because $T$ takes few values on a large sample, its ranked version is a step function with only $b+1$ levels, and its rank variance is a computable, highly structured quantity. Any response's correlation with $T$ is bounded by how much of that structure the response resolves.

Existing theory covers only the \emph{refining} case: if the response ranks strictly more finely than $T$, the classical tie-attenuation ceiling gives
\[
  \rho^2 \;\le\; \frac{\ssb}{(n^3-n)/12} \;=\; \frac67\Bigl(1+\frac{1}{n(n+1)}\Bigr) \;\longrightarrow\; \frac67,
\]
so $\rho \le 0.9258$, and the recorded $0.7192$ is well inside. But a \emph{rate} is not a refinement: a relation rate of $12.5\%$ is, in the simplest reading, a single indicator variable. This paper supplies the theory of coarse responses, then the theory of everything in between.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \S\ref{sec:coarse} --- the coarse-response ceiling, exact and attained, proved by a greedy-optimality argument requiring only two monotonicity inequalities and a counting identity; specialised to the dyadic profile it yields the rate parabola.
  \item \S\ref{sec:ladder} --- the nested-ties identity $\rho^2 = \ssb(\text{coarse})/\ssb(\text{fine})$ and monotonicity of coarsening, which order the entire resolution ladder and cap it at $1$.
  \item \S\ref{sec:bottom} --- exact bottom-blind ceilings at every dyadic depth, and a two-sided resolution threshold at the recorded measurement.
  \item \S\ref{sec:tip} --- the tip merge costs exactly the tip's own sum of squares (no interaction term), giving a ceiling above $7/8$ at every depth, hence a sharp bulk/tip asymmetry.
  \item \S\ref{sec:bitlen} --- a uniform $2\cdot 8^{-b}$ bound turning empirical scan-flatness into an algebraic fact.
  \item \S\ref{sec:geom} --- the geometric-ratio family: $\ssb = q(n^3-1)/(4(q^2+q+1))$, ceiling constant $C(q) = q+1+1/q$ strictly increasing, and the dyadic regime the hardest member. In particular $7/2$ is a shape constant, not an arithmetic one.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Setup: tie profiles, midranks, and the between-block sum of squares}

\subsection{Tie profiles}

\begin{definition}[Tie profile]
Let $T$ be a statistic on a sample of size $n$ taking finitely many values. Its \emph{tie profile} is the list $L = [m_0, \ldots, m_r]$ of block sizes, indexed in increasing order of the $T$-value, so $\sum_j m_j = n$. Block $j$ occupies rank positions $C_j+1, \ldots, C_j+m_j$, where $C_j = m_0 + \cdots + m_{j-1}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Midrank]
The midrank of block $j$ is $r_j = C_j + \tfrac{m_j+1}{2}$, the average rank position occupied by that block. The grand mean rank is $\mu = (n+1)/2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dyadic profile]
For the sample $\{0,1,\ldots,2^b-1\}$ the trailing-zero count has tie profile
\[
  D_b \;=\; \bigl[\,2^{b-1},\,2^{b-2},\,\ldots,\,2,\,1,\,1\,\bigr],
\]
with $b+1$ entries and $\sum D_b = 2^b$. Uniform draws of exact bit-length $48$ have uniform low $47$ bits, so the relevant profile is $D_{47}$ with $n = 2^{47}$.
\end{definition}

Throughout, ``bottom'' means low $T$ (the large blocks: half the sample is odd) and ``tip'' means high $T$ (the singletons).

\subsection{Centred rank mass and the between-block sum of squares}

\begin{definition}[Centred rank mass]
For a profile $L$ starting at rank offset $c$, $\massx_\mu(L,c) = \sum_j m_j (r_j - \mu)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Closed form]\label{lem:mass}
$\massx_\mu(L,c) = n\bigl(c + \tfrac{n+1}{2} - \mu\bigr)$ with $n = \sum L$. In particular $\massx_\mu(L,0) = 0$ when $\mu = (n+1)/2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $L$: the midrank mass of a block equals the raw rank mass of its members, since the midrank is their arithmetic mean; summing telescopes to the rank mass of the whole segment.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Between-block sum of squares]
$\ssb_\mu(L,c) = \sum_j m_j (r_j-\mu)^2$. We abbreviate $\ssb(L) := \ssb_{(n+1)/2}(L,0)$; this is the unnormalised variance $\sum_i (R_i-\mu)^2$ of the midrank vector of $T$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Parallel-axis algebra]\label{lem:pa}
$\ssb_\mu(L,c)$ depends on $(\mu,c)$ only through $\mu-c$, and
$\ssb_\mu(L,c) = \ssb_0(L,c) - 2\mu\,\massx_0(L,c) + \mu^2 n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Additivity]\label{lem:add}
$\ssb_\mu(L_1 \cat L_2, c) = \ssb_\mu(L_1,c) + \ssb_\mu\bigl(L_2, c+\textstyle\sum L_1\bigr)$.
\end{lemma}

Both are immediate inductions.

\subsection{The dyadic sum of squares}

\begin{theorem}[Dyadic sum of squares]\label{thm:ssdyadic}
For $b \ge 1$ and $n = 2^b$, $\displaystyle \ssb(D_b) = \frac{n^3-1}{14}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $b$ using Lemmas~\ref{lem:pa}--\ref{lem:add}. Writing $D_{b+1} = 2^{b} :: D_b$, the new leading block of size $2^{b}$ shifts the offset of the remainder by $2^{b}$ and moves the grand mean from $(2^{b}+1)/2$ to $(2^{b+1}+1)/2$; the parallel-axis cross term of the new block cancels against the recentring of the old ones, so level $k$ contributes exactly $8^{k}/2$. Hence $\ssb(D_b) = \tfrac12\sum_{k<b}8^{k} = \tfrac12\cdot\tfrac{8^b-1}{7} = \tfrac{n^3-1}{14}$.
\end{proof}

Comparing with the tie-free total $(n^3-n)/12$,
\[
  \frac{\ssb(D_b)}{(n^3-n)/12} \;=\; \frac{12(n^3-1)}{14(n^3-n)} \;=\; \frac67\Bigl(1+\frac{1}{n(n+1)}\Bigr),
\]
the classical tie-attenuation ceiling $\rho \le \sqrt{6/7} = 0.9258\ldots$ for any refining response.

\section{Coarse responses: the binary ceiling and the rate parabola}\label{sec:coarse}

\subsection{Selections and their moments}

\begin{definition}[Selection]
A \emph{selection} on $L = [m_0,\ldots,m_r]$ is a list $s = [s_0,\ldots,s_r]$ with $0 \le s_j \le m_j$, encoding a binary response marking $s_j$ of the $m_j$ members of block $j$. Which members are marked is immaterial: midranks are constant on blocks. Write $K = \sum s_j$, and $\selx_\mu(L,s,c) = \sum_j s_j(r_j-\mu)$.
\end{definition}

Selection mass is additive along concatenations of aligned profile/selection pairs, and a selection together with its pointwise complement carries the full mass: $\selx_\mu(L,s,c) + \selx_\mu(L,m-s,c) = \massx_\mu(L,c)$.

Let $n_1 = K$ and $n_0 = n-K$. Ranked, the response has two levels: marked items share the midrank $n_0 + \tfrac{n_1+1}{2}$, unmarked items share $\tfrac{n_0+1}{2}$.

\begin{lemma}[Variance]\label{lem:var}
$\Var = \sum_i (S_i-\mu)^2 = \dfrac{n\,K\,(n-K)}{4}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Cross moment]\label{thm:cov}
For any selection $s$ on $L$,
$\displaystyle \Cov = \sum_i (R_i-\mu)(S_i-\mu) = \frac n2\,\selx_\mu(L,s,0)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The two centred response midranks are $n_0/2$ (marked) and $-n_1/2$ (unmarked), since $n_0 + \tfrac{n_1+1}{2} - \tfrac{n+1}{2} = \tfrac{n_0}{2}$ and $\tfrac{n_0+1}{2} - \tfrac{n+1}{2} = -\tfrac{n_1}{2}$. Writing $M$ for the centred rank mass of the marked items, the unmarked items carry $-M$ because the total centred mass vanishes (Lemma~\ref{lem:mass}). Hence $\Cov = \tfrac{n_0}{2}M + (-\tfrac{n_1}{2})(-M) = \tfrac n2 M$.
\end{proof}

Thus the covariance depends on the response only through a \emph{linear} functional of the selection, while the variance depends only on the count. This is why a greedy argument suffices.

\subsection{Greedy optimality and the ceiling}

\begin{theorem}[Top-filling is optimal]\label{thm:greedy}
Split $L = L_1 \cat L_2$ at a block boundary and let $s = s_1 \cat s_2$ be any selection of total count $\sum L_2$. Then
$\selx_\mu(L,s,0) \le \massx_\mu\bigl(L_2, \sum L_1\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $W = \sum L_1$. Every block of $L_1$ finishes at or before $W$, so its centred midrank is at most $W-\mu$, giving $\selx(L_1,s_1) \le (\sum s_1)(W-\mu)$. Every block of $L_2$ starts at or after $W$, so its centred midrank is at least $W-\mu$, whence the unselected part of $L_2$ carries at least $(\sum L_2 - \sum s_2)(W-\mu)$. The count constraint gives $\sum s_1 = \sum L_2 - \sum s_2$, so the excess mass placed below the boundary is dominated by the mass abandoned above it.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
$\displaystyle \rho^2_{\mathrm{bin}}(L,s) = \frac{\Cov^2}{\ssb(L)\cdot\Var}$ is the squared Spearman coefficient between $T$ (profile $L$) and the binary response $s$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Coarse-response ceiling]\label{thm:coarse}
Let $L = L_1\cat L_2$ with $K = \sum L_1 > 0$ and $n-K = \sum L_2 > 0$, let $s$ be a selection of count $n-K$ with $\Cov \ge 0$, and suppose $\ssb(L) > 0$. Then
\[
  \rho^2_{\mathrm{bin}}(L,s) \;\le\; \frac{n\,K\,(n-K)}{4\,\ssb(L)}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:cov}, $\Cov = \tfrac n2 \selx$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:greedy} and Lemma~\ref{lem:mass}, $\selx \le (n-K)\bigl(K + \tfrac{n-K+1}{2} - \tfrac{n+1}{2}\bigr) = K(n-K)/2$. Hence $\Cov \le nK(n-K)/4$; square and divide by $\ssb\cdot\Var$ with $\Var = nK(n-K)/4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]
The top-filling response $s = [0,\ldots,0]\cat L_2$ attains the bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:coarse} with equality.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
The bound has $\ssb$ in the \emph{denominator}: it is antitone in the between-block variance of $T$, the reverse of the refining ceiling. This single sign change drives Theorems~\ref{thm:sixseven} and~\ref{thm:inversion}.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The rate parabola}

\begin{theorem}[Rate parabola]\label{thm:parabola}
On the dyadic profile $D_b$ with $n = 2^b$, the coarse ceiling at the aligned dyadic rate $p = 2^{-t}$ ($0 \le t \le b$) is
\[
  \rho^2_{\max}(p) \;=\; \frac72\,p\,(1-p)\,\frac{n^3}{n^3-1}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The prefix sums of $D_b$ are the dyadic boundaries: the first $t$ blocks sum to $2^b - 2^{b-t}$ and the rest to $2^{b-t}$. Substituting $K = (1-p)n$, $n-K = pn$ and $\ssb = (n^3-1)/14$ into Theorem~\ref{thm:coarse} gives $\tfrac{14\,p(1-p)n^3}{4(n^3-1)}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Value at the recorded rate]
At $b = 47$, $p = 1/8$,
\[
  \rho^2_{\max} = \frac{49}{128}\Bigl(1+\frac{1}{2^{141}-1}\Bigr) = 0.3828125\ldots, \qquad \rho_{\max} = \frac{7}{8\sqrt2} = 0.618718\ldots
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[No binary response at the recorded rate]
Every two-valued response at exact bit-length $48$ whose marked count matches the recorded $12.5\%$ boundary has $\rho^2 < 0.3829$, strictly below $0.7192^2 = 0.5172$, $0.7202^2$ and $0.7198^2$. The relation rate cannot be a single-trial indicator; it must be a graded response.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[A binary model needs double the rate]
For every dyadic rate $2^{-t}$ with $t \ge 3$ the coarse ceiling is below $0.39 < 0.5172$; at rate $1/4$ it is $21/32 = 0.65625 > 0.5172$. A two-valued response reproducing the measurement therefore requires a relation rate of at least $25\%$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The $6/7$ ceiling is not universal]\label{thm:sixseven}
For every $b \ge 3$,
$\displaystyle \frac67\Bigl(1+\frac{1}{n(n+1)}\Bigr) < \frac78\cdot\frac{n^3}{n^3-1}$.
Coarsening the response can therefore raise the attainable dial: it shrinks the response's own variance faster than the covariance. Numerically $6/7 = 0.857143$ versus $7/8 = 0.875$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Headroom inversion reverses]\label{thm:inversion}
Let $B_b$ denote the popcount tie profile on $b$ bits (block sizes $\binom{b}{k}$). At $b = 47$ one has $\ssb(D_{47}) < \ssb(B_{47})$, so the popcount statistic has strictly more refining headroom than $T$. Since the coarse ceiling is antitone in $\ssb$, for every aligned split $0 < K < n$,
\[
  \frac{nK(n-K)}{4\,\ssb(B_{47})} \;<\; \frac{nK(n-K)}{4\,\ssb(D_{47})},
\]
i.e.\ the count baseline's coarse ceiling is strictly below $T$'s. The recorded advantage of $T$ over the count baseline ($+0.098$ to $+0.145$) points the way the coarse theory predicts, and against the refining theory.
\end{theorem}

\section{The resolution ladder}\label{sec:ladder}

A relation rate need not be two-valued: it may be flat on the $87.5\%$ of draws where no relation occurs while resolving the remaining $12.5\%$ arbitrarily finely. The following law covers every partially-coarse response.

\begin{definition}[Nesting / coarsening]
A \emph{nesting} is a list of lists $\mathcal L = [G_1,\ldots,G_k]$ whose concatenation $\flatn(\mathcal L)$ is the fine profile of $T$. It represents a response constant on each group of consecutive tie blocks --- merging adjacent $T$-levels but never splitting or reordering them. Its own tie profile is $\coarse(\mathcal L) = [\sum G_1, \ldots, \sum G_k]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Midrank collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
The centred cross moment between the fine midranks of $T$ and the coarse midranks of the response equals the coarse between-group sum of squares:
\[
  \Cov \;=\; \sum_i \Bigl(c_i + \tfrac{\sum G_i + 1}{2} - \mu\Bigr)\massx_\mu(G_i, c_i) \;=\; \ssb_\mu\bigl(\coarse(\mathcal L), 0\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Inside group $i$ the coarse midrank is constant and factors out; the remaining factor is the group's own centred mass, which by Lemma~\ref{lem:mass} is $\lvert G_i\rvert$ times its centred midrank. The product is the group's contribution to the coarse sum of squares.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nested-ties law]\label{thm:nested}
For any nesting $\mathcal L$ with $\ssb(\coarse(\mathcal L)) > 0$,
\[
  \rho^2 \;=\; \frac{\ssb\bigl(\coarse(\mathcal L)\bigr)}{\ssb\bigl(\flatn(\mathcal L)\bigr)}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\rho^2 = \Cov^2/(\ssb(\text{fine})\cdot\ssb(\text{coarse}))$ by definition and $\Cov = \ssb(\text{coarse})$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}; one factor cancels.
\end{proof}

This is an identity, not a bound: the squared Spearman coefficient is exactly the fraction of the between-block variance of $T$ that survives the coarsening. Taking the coarse side to be a two-group split recovers the rate parabola; taking the fine side to be the tie-free ranking recovers $6/7$.

\begin{theorem}[Coarsening loses variance]\label{thm:mono}
$\ssb_\mu(\coarse(\mathcal L),c) \le \ssb_\mu(\flatn(\mathcal L),c)$ for every nesting; consequently $\rho^2 \le 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Group by group one needs $(\massx)^2/\lvert G\rvert \le \ssb(G)$, which is Cauchy--Schwarz (equivalently, the parallel-axis theorem: the group's internal spread is discarded). Lemma~\ref{lem:add} assembles the groups.
\end{proof}

The ladder is therefore ordered: finer responses attain more, and every rung is capped at $1$.

\section{Bottom-blindness is excluded}\label{sec:bottom}

\begin{definition}[Bottom-blind profile]
At depth $t$, $\mathrm{BM}(b,t) = \bigl[\sum(\text{first }t\text{ blocks of }D_b)\bigr] \cat D_{b-t}$: the lowest $t$ tie blocks (a fraction $1-2^{-t}$ of the sample) are merged into one group, everything above is fully resolved. By Theorem~\ref{thm:mono} this is the best response blind on the bottom $t$ blocks.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Bottom-blind sum of squares]\label{thm:bm}
For $t+1 \le b$,
\[
  \ssb\bigl(\mathrm{BM}(b,t)\bigr) \;=\; \frac{(2^t-1)2^t}{4}\,8^{\,b-t} \;+\; \frac{8^{\,b-t}-1}{14}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $N_1 = 2^b - 2^{b-t} = (2^t-1)2^{b-t}$. The merged bulk has midrank $(N_1+1)/2$, displaced from $\mu$ by $-2^{\,b-t-1}$, contributing $N_1 2^{2(b-t-1)} = (2^t-1)8^{\,b-t}/4$. The tail is a translate of $D_{b-t}$ at offset $N_1$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:pa} its contribution splits into the aggregate $2^{\,b-t}(N_1/2)^2 = (2^t-1)^2 8^{\,b-t}/4$ plus its own $\ssb(D_{b-t}) = (8^{\,b-t}-1)/14$. Adding aggregates, $\bigl[(2^t-1)+(2^t-1)^2\bigr]/4 = (2^t-1)2^t/4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bottom-blind ceiling]\label{thm:bmceil}
For $t+1 \le b$,
\[
  \rho^2 \;=\; \frac{\tfrac72(2^t-1)2^t\,8^{\,b-t} + 8^{\,b-t}-1}{8^{\,b}-1}.
\]
At $t=3$ this is $(197\cdot 8^{\,b-3}-1)/(8^{\,b}-1) \to 197/512 = 0.384766\ldots$; at $t=2$ it tends to $43/64 = 0.671875$; at $t=1$ it equals $1$ (no merge has occurred).
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Small for $t \ge 3$]
For all $t \ge 3$ and $b \ge t+1$ the bottom-blind ceiling is $< 0.45$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $u = 2^t \ge 8$ and $y = 8^{b-t} \ge 8$ the ceiling is $(\tfrac72(u-1)uy + y - 1)/(u^3y-1)$, and $\tfrac72 u(u-1)+1 \le \tfrac{45}{100}u^3$ for $u \ge 8$ (at $u=8$: $197 \le 230.4$), which suffices after clearing denominators.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bottom-blindness excluded]
At exact bit-length $48$, every response that fails to distinguish inside the bottom $t \ge 3$ tie blocks of $T$ --- i.e.\ flat on at least the $87.5\%$ no-relation bulk --- satisfies $\rho^2 \le 0.3848 < 0.5172 = 0.7192^2$, however finely it resolves the remaining $12.5\%$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Resolution threshold, two-sided]
At $b = 47$: for every $t \ge 3$ the depth-$t$ bottom-blind ceiling is strictly below $0.7192^2$ (excluded); at $t = 2$ the ceiling is $\approx 0.6719 > 0.7192^2$ (not excluded).
\end{theorem}

The information certified by the measured correlation therefore does not live on the relation events: the mechanism must carry graded structure across the $87.5\%$ of draws where nothing is observed to happen.

\section{Tip-blindness is admissible}\label{sec:tip}

\begin{definition}[Tip-blind profile]
$\mathrm{TM}(b,t) = (\text{first }t\text{ blocks of }D_b) \cat [\,2^{\,b-t}\,]$: full resolution on the bottom $t$ blocks, one single group for the entire top $2^{-t}$ fraction of the $T$-scale.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The tip merge has no interaction term]\label{thm:tip}
For $t \le b$ with $b-t \ge 1$,
\[
  \ssb\bigl(\mathrm{TM}(b,t)\bigr) \;=\; \ssb(D_b) \;-\; \frac{8^{\,b-t}-1}{14},
\]
i.e.\ merging the tip costs exactly the merged part's own between-block sum of squares.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:add} the two profiles agree on the first $t$ blocks. On the tail, the merged group's parallel-axis contribution $\lvert\cdot\rvert(\bar r-\mu)^2$ coincides identically with the aggregate term of the fine tail, since the fine tail's centred mass about its own mean vanishes (Lemma~\ref{lem:mass}). What remains is the tail's internal $\ssb(D_{b-t}) = (8^{\,b-t}-1)/14$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tip-blind ceiling]
$\displaystyle \rho^2 = \frac{8^{\,b}-8^{\,b-t}}{8^{\,b}-1}$, and this exceeds $7/8$ for every $t \ge 1$, $b \ge t+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Divide Theorem~\ref{thm:tip} by $\ssb(D_b) = (8^b-1)/14$. For the bound, $8\cdot 8^{\,b-t} \le 8^{\,b}$ when $t \ge 1$, so $8^{\,b}-8^{\,b-t} \ge \tfrac78 8^{\,b} > \tfrac78(8^{\,b}-1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bulk/tip asymmetry]
At $b = 47$,
\[
  \rho^2\bigl(\mathrm{BM}(47,3)\bigr) = 0.3848 \;<\; 0.7192^2 \;<\; 0.8750 = \rho^2\bigl(\mathrm{TM}(47,1)\bigr).
\]
Merging the bottom $87.5\%$ destroys the dial; merging the top $50\%$ does not.
\end{theorem}

The asymmetry follows from where the between-block mass sits: in the geometric spectrum the level-$k$ contribution is $8^k/2$, concentrating mass overwhelmingly on the large, low-$T$ blocks. Tip blocks are extreme in rank but negligible in weight, and rank correlation is a mass-weighted average.

\section{Bit-length invariance}\label{sec:bitlen}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform bit-length bound]
For every $b \ge 1$ and every $p \in [0,1]$, with $n = 2^b$,
\[
  \Bigl\lvert\, \tfrac72 p(1-p)\tfrac{n^3}{n^3-1} \;-\; \tfrac72 p(1-p) \,\Bigr\rvert \;\le\; \frac{2}{8^{\,b}}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The difference is $\tfrac72 p(1-p)/(n^3-1)$; with $p(1-p) \le 1/4$ and $n^3 \ge 8$ this is at most $\tfrac78/(n^3-1) \le 2/n^3$.
\end{proof}

At $b = 47$ the bound is $7\times 10^{-43}$. Every ceiling above therefore coincides with the limit parabola $\tfrac72 p(1-p)$ to exponential accuracy: the constraint landscape is self-similar in bit-length, because increasing $b$ by one adds a single new block at the mass-negligible tip. The empirical flatness of the measurement across bit-length scans is an algebraic fact about the tie spectrum, not a property of the sampler.

\section{The geometric-ratio family: $7/2$ is a shape constant}\label{sec:geom}

Nothing above used the primality of $2$, divisibility, or any arithmetic property --- only the geometric shape of the tie profile.

\begin{definition}[Ratio-$1/q$ profile]
For a digit base $q \ge 2$, the trailing-zero-digit statistic on $\{0,\ldots,q^b-1\}$ has tie profile
\[
  G_q(b) = \bigl[\,(q-1)q^{b-1},\,(q-1)q^{b-2},\,\ldots,\,(q-1),\,1\,\bigr], \qquad \textstyle\sum G_q(b) = q^b.
\]
At $q = 2$ this is exactly $D_b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric-ratio sum of squares]\label{thm:geomss}
With $n = q^b$,
\[
  \ssb\bigl(G_q(b)\bigr) \;=\; \frac{q\,(n^3-1)}{4\,(q^2+q+1)}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $b$ with the parallel-axis identity, as in Theorem~\ref{thm:ssdyadic}. Prepending the block of size $(q-1)q^{b-1}$ shifts the offset of the old profile by that amount and moves the grand mean from $(q^b+1)/2$ to $(q^{b+1}+1)/2$; the cross term of the new block cancels against the recentring of the old ones, so level $k$ contributes exactly $q^{3k}\,q(q-1)/4$. Summing,
$\tfrac{q(q-1)}{4}\cdot\tfrac{q^{3b}-1}{q^3-1} = \tfrac{q(n^3-1)}{4(q^2+q+1)}$, since $q^3-1 = (q-1)(q^2+q+1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
At $q=2$, $\ssb(D_b) = 2(n^3-1)/(4\cdot 7) = (n^3-1)/14$, recovering Theorem~\ref{thm:ssdyadic}. (Check at $q=3$, $b=1$: blocks $[2,1]$, midranks $1.5, 3$, mean $2$, giving $2(0.5)^2+1 = 3/2$; the formula gives $3\cdot 26/(4\cdot 13) = 3/2$.)
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric-ratio ceiling]
For a two-valued response at relation rate $p$ on the ratio-$1/q$ spectrum with $n = q^b$, $b \ge 1$,
\[
  \rho^2_{\max}(p) \;=\; \frac{n \cdot pn \cdot (1-p)n}{4\,\ssb(G_q(b))} \;=\; C(q)\,p\,(1-p)\,\frac{n^3}{n^3-1}, \qquad C(q) := \frac{q^2+q+1}{q}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Strict monotonicity of the ceiling constant]
For $1 \le x < y$, $\displaystyle \frac{x^2+x+1}{x} < \frac{y^2+y+1}{y}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Cross-multiplying, the claim is $0 < (y-x)(xy-1)$, which holds since $y > x \ge 1$ forces $xy > 1$. Equivalently $C(q) = q+1+1/q$ has derivative $1-1/q^2 > 0$ for $q > 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The dyadic regime is the hardest]
For every $q > 2$ and every $p \in (0,1)$, $\;\tfrac72 p(1-p) < C(q)\,p(1-p)$.
\end{theorem}

Hence $7/2$ is a \emph{shape} constant: it measures the geometric ratio of the tie spectrum, not the prime $2$. Written $C(q) = q+1+1/q$, it is minimised over integer bases at $q=2$. Coarser digit bases decay more slowly and make the dial easier to saturate: $C(2)=3.5$, $C(3)=4.33$, $C(4)=5.25$, $C(8)=9.125$, with limiting ceilings at $p=1/8$ of $0.383$, $0.474$, $0.574$, $0.998$. Every exclusion in this paper is therefore the strongest statement available anywhere in the geometric family; a base-$8$ analogue could not exclude a binary indicator at rate $1/8$ at all.

\section{Algorithms}

All quantities here are exactly computable in rational arithmetic.

\paragraph{Algorithm A --- between-block sum of squares.} Given $L = [m_0,\ldots,m_r]$, sweep once maintaining the prefix sum $C$, emitting $r_j = C + (m_j+1)/2$ and accumulating $m_j(r_j-\mu)^2$ with $\mu = (n+1)/2$. Time $O(r)$; the closed forms of Theorems~\ref{thm:ssdyadic} and~\ref{thm:geomss} reduce this to $O(1)$ for geometric profiles.

\paragraph{Algorithm B --- ceiling evaluator.} Given a nesting as a list of group boundaries, form the coarse profile by prefix-summing within groups, apply Algorithm A to both profiles, and return the ratio. By Theorem~\ref{thm:nested} this returns the exact attainable $\rho^2$, not a bound. Time $O(r)$.

\paragraph{Algorithm C --- exhaustive sharpness certificate.} For small $b$, enumerate all selections with $0 \le s_j \le m_j$ and $\sum s_j = K$, evaluate $\rho^2_{\mathrm{bin}}$ via $\Cov = \tfrac n2\sum_j s_j(r_j-\mu)$ and $\Var = nK(n-K)/4$, and compare the maximum with the closed form. Complexity $O\bigl(\prod_j(m_j+1)\bigr)$, so a small-$b$ certificate only. At $(b,t) \in \{(3,1),(4,1),(4,2),(5,2)\}$ it reproduces $64/73$, $512/585$, $128/195$, $3072/4681$ exactly, in each case with the top-segment maximiser.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What a single scalar buys}

A single rank correlation measured against a heavily-tied statistic is a constraint on the resolution structure of an unobserved mechanism. Theorem~\ref{thm:nested} converts the scalar into an exact statement about how much block variance the mechanism must preserve, permanently ruling out named families. Ties are usually treated as a defect to be corrected by an attenuation factor; here the tie spectrum is the instrument, and its mass distribution determines which parts of the scale a strong correlation certifies. For a geometric spectrum mass concentrates at the low end, so a strong correlation certifies fine structure in the bulk and says almost nothing about the tail.

\subsection{Why coarse and fine ceilings point opposite ways}

The refining ceiling is increasing in $\ssb$; the coarse ceiling is decreasing in it. A heavily-tied statistic has low $\ssb$, hence a low refining ceiling but a high coarse ceiling. This drives both $7/8 > 6/7$ and the baseline reversal of Theorem~\ref{thm:inversion}: when comparing candidate predictors against a coarse target, the ranking by headroom must be recomputed.

\subsection{Scope and limitations}

Theorem~\ref{thm:greedy} is stated for counts matching a block boundary; for intermediate rates the same argument bounds at the nearest boundaries, with the parabola interpolating (the recorded rate $1/8 = 2^{-3}$ is exactly aligned). Theorem~\ref{thm:nested} requires constancy on \emph{consecutive} blocks; a response merging non-adjacent levels or reversing order is bounded above by the corresponding consecutive coarsening. All exclusions are exclusions of response \emph{shapes}: they delimit the set of mechanisms compatible with the measurement without identifying one. The margins are large ($0.5172$ against a $0.3848$ cap), so the conclusions are robust to substantial revision of the measured value.

