\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{Valuation-class resolution spectrum.} The bottom-blind and tip-blind families are the two extreme one-parameter slices of the lattice of all consecutive coarsenings of the $b+1$ tie levels, ordered by refinement. By Theorem~\ref{thm:nested} each element has an exactly computable $\rho^2$, so the measurement induces a cut through the lattice. Characterising that cut combinatorially would upgrade the two threshold results into a complete classification; since the level-$k$ block contributes $8^k/2$ to $(n^3-1)/14$, the cut ought to be describable by a weight condition on the merged index sets alone.

\paragraph{Non-geometric spectra.} Theorem~\ref{thm:geomss} covers ratio-$1/q$ spectra. The popcount profile $\binom{b}{k}$ is the natural non-geometric comparison; a general formula for $\ssb$ in terms of the profile's generating function would place all such statistics in one frame and identify which shape constants are attainable.

\paragraph{Interpolation between boundaries.} Extending greedy optimality to non-aligned rates would give the exact ceiling at arbitrary $p$, presumably a piecewise-quadratic interpolation pinned at the dyadic boundaries.

\paragraph{Two-sided design.} The ceilings are constructive --- the top-filling response attains the coarse bound, and the maximally-fine-above-the-line response attains the bottom-blind bound --- so they double as design targets: given a resolution budget, they say exactly which merges to spend it on. For a geometric spectrum, spend everything on the bulk.

\paragraph{Lower-bound theory.} All results here are ceilings. A complementary theory of floors --- the minimum correlation forced by a given resolution structure --- would let a measured value pin a mechanism from both sides, converting exclusions into a confidence region on the resolution profile.

